{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And YHWH descended, etc., and spoke to him.</b> One needs to know what YHWH spoke to him. Further, one needs to know why all this was necessary and why He did not simply cause them to prophesy without all this preparation. The matter may be explained according to the statement of the Sages, of blessed memory, in the Zohar (Pinchas 232a) on the verse ״לָכֵן אֱמוֹר הִנְנִי נוֹתֵן לוֹ אֶת בְּרִיתִי שָׁלוֹם״ — for YHWH commanded Moses to say ״הִנְנִי נוֹתֵן״, etc., and the reason is because Moses had already merited the covenant of peace and it was considered his, and when He wished to give it to Phinehas, it turned out that He had already given it to Moses and he had merited it, and the Holy One, blessed be He, does not take back His gifts. So too the gift of prophecy — He gave to Moses as a portion, and all who come to prophesy, it is Moses who gives [prophecy] to them for the reason mentioned above, and therefore he is called the master of the prophets. And this is the statement of YHWH here, that He commanded to gather the elders with him present, ״and YHWH descended in the cloud.״ And His saying <b>and spoke to him</b> — meaning the language of dominion and rulership, as in the expression (Psalms 47:4) ״יַדְבֵּר עַמִּים תַּחְתֵּינוּ״ — that YHWH established dominion through this word to him, meaning to Moses, and it was within his authority and will to bestow, etc." "<b>And he bestowed of the spirit.</b> His saying <b>וַיָּאצֶל</b> — perhaps it alludes to the source of the spirit of prophecy that is in this aspect, and in this manner the use of וַיָּאצֶל is justified. And His saying <b>מִן הָרוּחַ אֲשֶׁר עָלָיו</b> — meaning, from that aspect which is upon him; but the bestowal comes from the Bestower, blessed be He, and not that He takes out from Moses to give to others. Or it may say <b>מִן הָרוּחַ</b> — which is upon Moses, and He said <b>וַיָּאצֵל</b> for the reason that there is no distinction between the spirit that remains upon him and the spirit given from him to the elders. And according to this approach, the expression <b>אֲשֶׁר עָלָיו</b> refers to the bestowed portion itself, which still remains upon him after וַיָּאָצֵל. And according to the words of the Midrash (Numbers Rabbah 15:19), when He counted the additional levels that the two [Eldad and Medad] had over the seventy, He also counted that the seventy's prophecy was from Moses' [allotment] while the two's was from the Holy One, blessed be He." "<b>And it came to pass, when it rested.</b> He said <b>וַיְהִי</b> — an expression of distress, according to the Sages, of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 15:19), that the elders prophesied the utterance ״הִתְקַדְּשׁוּ לְמָחָר״ — there is no greater distress than this, that at the very beginning of their prophecy they told Israel to prepare themselves for punishment." "And His saying ״<b>וַיִּתְנַבְּאוּ</b>״ with the addition of the vav — to say that besides the fact that they were prepared vessels for the spirit to rest upon them, they additionally prophesied. And Scripture informs us that their prophecy was immediate, as His saying ״when it rested upon them, etc., and they prophesied.״ Further, it may be explained according to the reasoning of those who say ״וְלֹא יָסָפוּ״ — that they did not prophesy except that one day — on this basis, ״וַיְהִי כְּנוֹחַ״, etc., is an expression of distress as mentioned, and there is yet another distress for them: that they prophesied and did not continue."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:07:58.444572526Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}